                  "caption": null
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "endotracheal_tube_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "Introduction",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "endotracheal_tube_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An endotracheal tube (ETT) is a tube inserted through the mouth or nose into the trachea to establish and maintain a secure airway. It allows oxygen delivery, ventilation and, in selected circumstances, airway protection."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It is commonly used during general anaesthesia, resuscitation, intensive care and emergency airway management."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Key OSCE identification point: A flexible, curved tube with a standard 15 mm connector at the proximal end, depth markings along the shaft, and a distal bevel. Many models have an inflatable cuff near the distal end and a pilot balloon connected to the cuff."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Unlike a laryngoscope, which helps visualise the vocal cords, the ETT is the device that establishes the tracheal airway."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "endotracheal_tube_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "Classification",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "endotracheal_tube_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "ETTs may be classified according to:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Route: Oral or nasal."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Cuff: Cuffed or uncuffed."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Material: Commonly medical-grade PVC or silicone in specialised models."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Design: Standard, reinforced, preformed, double-lumen or specialised tubes."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Purpose: Routine ventilation, difficult airway management, lung isolation or other specific clinical indications."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The standard cuffed single-lumen ETT is the principal design to recognise in a general OSCE."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "endotracheal_tube_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "Parts and labelling",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "endotracheal_tube_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/endotracheal_tube_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "mdpi.com"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Part",
                    "Function"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Proximal connector",
                      "Connects the tube to a breathing circuit or ventilation device; commonly 15 mm."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Tube shaft",
                      "Provides the passage for airflow."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Depth markings",
                      "Indicate insertion depth along the tube."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Vocal cord marker",
                      "Helps guide positioning relative to the vocal cords in designs that include it."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Bevel",
                      "Angled distal opening that facilitates passage through the glottis."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Murphy eye",
                      "Additional side opening near the distal end that may permit gas flow if the main opening becomes obstructed."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Inflatable cuff",
                      "Forms a seal against the tracheal wall when inflated."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Inflation line",
                      "Carries air between the pilot balloon assembly and the cuff."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Pilot balloon",
                      "Indicates cuff inflation and permits access to the inflation system."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Inflation valve",
                      "Allows cuff inflation and deflation using an appropriate syringe."
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important: Not every ETT has a cuff, a Murphy eye or a dedicated vocal cord marker. Identify the features actually present on the specimen."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "endotracheal_tube_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "Types of endotracheal tubes",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "endotracheal_tube_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/endotracheal_tube_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "A. Cuffed ETT"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Has an inflatable cuff near the distal end."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Helps seal the trachea and reduce leakage during ventilation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Commonly used in adults and children when clinically appropriate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Cuff pressure must be monitored to reduce tracheal mucosal injury."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/endotracheal_tube_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "B. Uncuffed ETT"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Has no inflatable cuff."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Traditionally used frequently in paediatric airway management."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Modern appropriately designed cuffed tubes are also widely used in children."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Tube size and fit must be selected carefully."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/endotracheal_tube_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "C. Reinforced or armoured ETT"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Contains a reinforcing spiral to resist kinking."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Useful in selected operations where tube bending is likely."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Cannot be shortened or cut indiscriminately; follow the manufacturer's instructions."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Can still kink, become compressed or be damaged."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/endotracheal_tube_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "D. Preformed RAE tube"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Has a predetermined curve that directs the circuit away from the operative field."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Oral RAE tubes are commonly used in selected oral and facial operations."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Nasal RAE tubes are used in selected procedures requiring nasal intubation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Positioning and length must be checked carefully."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/endotracheal_tube_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "E. Double-lumen tube"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Has separate lumens designed to ventilate the lungs independently."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Used for lung isolation during selected thoracic operations."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Requires appropriate sizing and confirmation of position."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "More specialised than a standard single-lumen ETT."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Other specialised designs include microlaryngeal tubes, laser-resistant tubes, subglottic suction tubes and tubes designed for specific difficult-airway techniques."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "endotracheal_tube_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "Principle of operation",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "endotracheal_tube_group_6",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The ETT creates a conduit between the respiratory circuit and the trachea."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The tube is passed through the vocal cords under appropriate visualisation or by a suitable alternative technique."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The distal tip is positioned within the trachea, above the carina."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "In a cuffed tube, the cuff is inflated to achieve an appropriate seal."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The proximal connector is attached to the ventilation circuit."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Tube position is confirmed, and ventilation is monitored."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The cuff helps reduce gas leakage and gross aspiration around the tube, but it does not completely prevent aspiration."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "endotracheal_tube_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "Sizes and selection",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "endotracheal_tube_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "ETT size is generally specified by the internal diameter (ID) in millimetres. The external diameter also matters because it determines how much space the tube occupies within the airway."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Approximate reference sizes:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Patient group",
                    "Typical ETT internal diameter"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Average adult female",
                      "7.0–7.5 mm"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Average adult male",
                      "7.5–8.5 mm"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Neonate",
                      "Approximately 2.5–3.5 mm, depending on weight and gestational age"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Infant and child",
                      "Selected according to age, anatomy and tube design"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "These are starting references, not universal prescriptions. Patient anatomy, clinical indication and manufacturer specifications determine the final choice."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "For children, age-based formulas can help estimate size, but they should not replace paediatric airway guidance. Cuffed and uncuffed tube sizing may differ."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "endotracheal_tube_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "Depth markings and placement",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "endotracheal_tube_group_8",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The markings along the ETT shaft indicate the distance from the distal tip. They help the clinician assess and document insertion depth at the teeth, lips or nares, depending on the route and protocol."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The tube must be positioned:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Beyond the vocal cords."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Within the trachea."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Above the carina, with adequate clearance to reduce endobronchial placement."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "At an appropriate depth for the patient's size and anatomy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A tube inserted too far may enter the right main bronchus, causing preferential ventilation of the right lung and reduced ventilation of the left lung."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A tube that is too shallow may become displaced or extubated."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Never rely on the depth marking alone to confirm tracheal placement."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "endotracheal_tube_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "Indications and clinical uses",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "endotracheal_tube_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "General anaesthesia when a secured airway is required."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Respiratory failure requiring invasive mechanical ventilation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Cardiac arrest and selected resuscitation situations."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Inability to maintain or protect the airway in selected patients."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Severe impairment of consciousness with inadequate airway protection, depending on the clinical assessment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Selected operations requiring controlled ventilation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Lung isolation during thoracic surgery when a specialised tube is indicated."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The decision to intubate depends on the patient's physiology, airway assessment, clinical context and available alternatives."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "endotracheal_tube_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "endotracheal_tube_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Provides a secure airway for ventilation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Allows delivery of oxygen and anaesthetic gases."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Supports controlled mechanical ventilation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Facilitates suctioning of tracheal secretions."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A cuffed tube can reduce leakage and gross contamination of the lower airway."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Allows capnography through a compatible breathing circuit."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Specialised designs support specific surgical and airway requirements."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "endotracheal_tube_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "Disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "endotracheal_tube_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Requires appropriate skill and equipment for safe insertion."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "May cause airway trauma and vocal cord injury."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Can provoke cardiovascular responses during insertion."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Cuff pressure can damage tracheal mucosa if excessive."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "May become obstructed by secretions, kinking or biting."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Can be displaced or inserted into the oesophagus or a main bronchus."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Requires monitoring and ongoing airway care."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "endotracheal_tube_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "Complications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "endotracheal_tube_group_12",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Complication",
                    "Mechanism or consequence"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Oesophageal intubation",
                      "The tube enters the oesophagus rather than the trachea, resulting in ineffective ventilation."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Endobronchial intubation",
                      "The tube advances too far, commonly into the right main bronchus."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Hypoxaemia",
                      "May result from failed placement, displacement, obstruction or inadequate ventilation."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Dental and oral trauma",
                      "Injury during laryngoscopy or tube passage."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Laryngeal injury",
                      "Trauma to the vocal cords or surrounding structures."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Tracheal mucosal injury",
                      "Excessive cuff pressure or prolonged pressure."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Aspiration",
                      "Gastric contents enter the airway despite attempted protection."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Tube obstruction",
                      "Secretions, kinking, biting or device malfunction."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Accidental extubation",
                      "Tube displacement or inadequate fixation."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Post-intubation sore throat or hoarseness",
                      "Common minor symptoms; persistent symptoms need assessment."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Tracheal stenosis",
                      "Potential late complication of significant airway injury or prolonged pressure."
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "endotracheal_tube_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "Pre-use checks",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "endotracheal_tube_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Before use, inspect the following:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Tube integrity: Ensure there are no cracks, kinks, defects or obstructions."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Correct size: Confirm the internal diameter and suitability for the patient."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Connector: Check that it fits the intended breathing circuit securely."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Cuff: Inflate and check the cuff and inflation system according to the manufacturer's instructions, then deflate it before insertion."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Pilot balloon and valve: Check for leakage or damage."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Murphy eye and distal tip: Confirm that openings are patent and the tip is undamaged."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Depth markings: Ensure they are visible."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Equipment readiness: Prepare suction, ventilation equipment, a means of confirming placement and an appropriate rescue airway plan."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "endotracheal_tube_subsection_14",
          "subsection_title": "How to confirm ETT placement",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "endotracheal_tube_group_14",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "1. Continuous waveform capnography"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The principal method for confirming tracheal placement in standard circumstances. A sustained exhaled carbon dioxide waveform supports tracheal placement when interpreted in the clinical context."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "2. Chest movement and auscultation"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Assess chest expansion and breath sounds on both sides, and listen over the epigastrium where appropriate. These are supplementary assessments, not substitutes for waveform capnography."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "3. Oxygenation and ventilation monitoring"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Monitor oxygen saturation, exhaled carbon dioxide, airway pressures and delivered tidal volumes as appropriate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "4. Further assessment when indicated"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Review tube depth and use imaging when appropriate to assess depth or investigate suspected complications. A chest X-ray may help identify endobronchial placement but is not a substitute for immediate confirmation of tracheal placement."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Emergency rule: If oesophageal placement is suspected, do not continue ineffective ventilation while relying on chest movement or auscultation alone. Follow the appropriate airway emergency algorithm and prioritise effective oxygenation."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "endotracheal_tube_subsection_15",
          "subsection_title": "Cuff pressure: important OSCE topic",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "endotracheal_tube_group_15",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The cuff seals against the tracheal wall. Excessive pressure can impair mucosal blood flow and cause injury."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Measure cuff pressure using an appropriate cuff manometer."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A commonly used adult target is approximately 20–30 cm H₂O, adjusted according to the clinical context and local protocol."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Check for an adequate seal without overinflating the cuff."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Reassess after repositioning, transfer or any event that may affect tube position or cuff integrity."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Cuff inflation volume alone is not a reliable measure of cuff pressure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An inflated cuff does not guarantee protection against aspiration, particularly when secretions accumulate above it."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "endotracheal_tube_subsection_16",
          "subsection_title": "High-yield OSCE viva questions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "endotracheal_tube_group_16",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. Identify this instrument."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An endotracheal tube, used to establish a tracheal airway for ventilation and oxygen delivery."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What is the function of the Murphy eye?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It provides an additional opening for gas flow if the main distal opening becomes obstructed."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. What is the function of the cuff?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It creates a seal against the tracheal wall to reduce gas leakage and gross aspiration around the tube."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. What is the function of the pilot balloon?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It indicates the state of the cuff inflation system and provides access for cuff inflation and deflation. It does not directly measure cuff pressure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. What is the usual connector size?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The standard proximal ETT connector is generally 15 mm."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. What does an ETT size of 7.5 mm refer to?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It refers to the internal diameter of the tube in millimetres."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. What happens if the tube is advanced too far?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It may enter a main bronchus, commonly the right, causing endobronchial intubation and potentially reduced ventilation of the opposite lung."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q8. What is the most important routine method of confirming tracheal placement?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Continuous waveform capnography, interpreted alongside the patient's clinical condition."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q9. Can the cuff prevent all aspiration?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. It reduces leakage around the tube but does not eliminate aspiration risk."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q10. What is the danger of excessive cuff pressure?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It can impair tracheal mucosal perfusion and cause tissue injury, ulceration or, in severe cases, later stenosis."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
